  "name": "INSULIN FASTING",
  "code": "1770",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\"><strong>COMMENTS:</strong></span><br><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">Stimulation of insulin secretion can be caused by many factors including hyperglycemia, glucagon, and amino acids and by complex mechanisms involving growth hormone or catecholamines. Immunoassays for insulin have been widely used to provide supplementary information, for the diagnosis of diabetes mellitus and for differential diagnosis of fasting hypoglycemia to discriminate between insulinoma and factitious hypoglycemia. In this application the ratio of immune-reactive insulin to blood glucose (I/G) valuable than the insulin level&nbsp;alone.&nbsp;</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\"><strong>Increased level:</strong></span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">1. Obesity</span><br><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">2. Cushing&rsquo;s syndrome</span><br><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">3. Oral contraceptives</span><br><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">4. Acromegaly</span><br><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">5. Insulinoma</span><br><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">6. Hyperthyroidism&nbsp;</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\"><strong>Decreased level:</strong></span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">Overt diabetes mellitus&nbsp;</span></p>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">&nbsp; &nbsp;<strong> &nbsp;LIMITATIONS:</strong></span></p>\n<ol>\n<li style=\"font-size: 11px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">Specimens from patients treated with bovine or porcine insulin may interfere.</span></li>\n<li style=\"font-size: 11px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">Low levels may be measured in patients with insulin autoimmune syndrome or familial high pro insulinemia.</span></li>\n<li style=\"font-size: 11px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11px;\">False positive or depressed values are observed in patients who have received preparations of mouse monoclonal antibodies for&nbsp;diagnosis or therapy.&nbsp;</span></li>\n</ol>",
